A||||13||Mr.Frosty||How do I remove deck lid torsion springs?||||1||
Z||000000||Mr.Frosty||06-11-2001||11:13 AM||||I'm going to install gas struts for the decklid for more room and want to ditch those failing decklid springs.Is the best way to leave the lid on and remove the springs or do the wood block against the window thing?<P>------------------<BR>85 GT Turbo intercooled||||reg||1
Z||000001||88CoupeV6||06-11-2001||11:57 AM||||Whatever you do be careful, You could lose a hand with one of those things. They have a lot of tension in them!<P>------------------<BR>Josh <B>(known by TheFiero64 for first 1239 posts)</B><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg">    <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||4
Z||000002||Steve Normington||06-11-2001||01:20 PM||||Either method will work. I prefer to remove the decklid first. If you leave it on, the springs will wonk into it when you remove the Torx bolt and could chip/crack the deck. Also, that way you don't have to worry about the deck coming down on your head. As long as you have a piece of wood on the window (I use 12" x 12" plywood), you shouldn't crack the window. If your springs are colored white, be very careful, because these are the really strong springs. I assume you have a tamper proof Torx bit. Finally, mark the location of the hinge on the lid so you can realign it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mr.Frosty||06-11-2001||01:39 PM||||Thanks,I didn't think about the lid.I'm curious,can I bind the rods in the center together tightly with duct tape,and then cut them along the bar with a high speed or a torch and let them fly,or does that sound dangerous?Wouldn't they just spin in place real fast?<P>------------------<BR>85 GT Turbo intercooled||||reg||5
Z||000004||cadaver||06-11-2001||10:13 PM||||If you release the tension first they dont go through the window. Well that is if you do it right.<BR>You said "failing" I have never seen one of these springs fail?||||reg||1
Z||000005||dr fritz||06-11-2001||11:20 PM||||One of the few things that rarely fail   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||isthiswhereiputausername?||06-12-2001||12:00 AM||||I saved myself the hassle and risk of hurting something and grinded the  rod off  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Mr.Frosty||06-12-2001||10:04 AM||||The only reason I said failing is that when I'm working on the engine compartment,the lid constantly falls on my head.Is that not failing springs?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>85 GT Turbo intercooled||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mr.Frosty||06-12-2001||10:09 AM||||Come to think of it when my car is finished I plan on removing the GT wing and putting a reverse scoop on it.Then I'm sure it will fly open with gusto and then the restraint straps will start wearing.Oh well I still need more clearance for the turbo.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>85 GT Turbo intercooled||||reg||1
Z||000009||Steve Normington||06-12-2001||11:39 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mr.Frosty:<BR><B>I'm curious,can I bind the rods in the center together tightly with duct tape,and then cut them along the bar with a high speed or a torch and let them fly,or does that sound dangerous?Wouldn't they just spin in place real fast?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I think what will happen if you do this is that both ends of the springs will snap back when the tension is released. So the hinges will snap down and the other end will fly towards the trunk. You might want to wrap the lower hinge with tape to keep things from flying about.<P>As to the failing, they don't break, but they do weaken.<P>PS. If these are white springs, please don't cut them. White springs are very hard to find.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||NIFE [PaulV]||06-12-2001||12:02 PM||||Some years ago, Jim Hallman & I wrote a tech tip article on replacing the torque rods. We have used this method several times and works for us. It does take a helper to remove the holding pin, and it does require someone with some strength to release the torsion, but you can find the tip at:<BR> <A HREF="http://home.xnet.com/~paulv/techtips.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://home.xnet.com/~paulv/techtips.htm</A> <P>JUst click on Torque rod replacement||||reg||1
Z||000011||cadaver||06-12-2001||09:59 PM||||Frosty try adjusting them to a stiffer setting.<BR>Is there a wing or scoop where there was not one before?<BR>As for needing two people to remove the springs and having them fly up and kill the window I must be doing something wrong as I have always done it myself and never hit the window. Its not that hard a thing to do.||||reg||1
Z||000012||gtcash||06-13-2001||12:38 AM||||Tell me about the gas struts... I want em!<BR>I've seen them, what size? I took my torque rods off last night, I used a 2X2 4'long as a wedge...and a piece of plywood on the rear window...no problems, all by myself, wish I had had a friend to help lift off the deck lid tho, it was auckword as hell.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Philphine||06-13-2001||11:23 AM||||they can break. i don't know how or when it happened but i was raising the lid on my indy and noticing it seemed heavy. my drivers side spring had broken. i have a gas strut on my '87 so i'll probaly do the same to this car and toss the springs.<P>------------------<BR>Phil T<P>'87 coupe (feral)<BR>'84 indy<BR>'84 coupe (cheap thrill)<BR>'84 chop top (project. or at least an interesting collection of parts)||||reg||1
